<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahón</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Maó, Port Mahon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahón, or Maó, is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, set at the head of one of the largest natural harbours in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Known in antiquity as Mago, it is traditionally said to have been founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>, and in 418 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Severus described the forced conversion of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Its harbour made it a prize for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which held Menorca for much of the 18th century and made Mahón the island's capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahon.jpg" alt="Mahón" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jbr · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahon_Hafen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
